 "article": {
  "title": "Who Will Care for the Carers?",
  "topic": "社会民生·老龄照护",
  "level": "CET-4",
  "words": 310,
  "paras": [
   "By 2050, one in six people in the world will be over the age of 65, according to the United Nations — up from about one in eleven in 2019. In some countries the change is happening even faster. The result is a simple question that no society has fully answered: who will look after all these older people?",
   "For now, the honest answer is families — and especially women. Studies suggest that the great majority of long-term care is unpaid work done at home by daughters, wives and, increasingly, sons. These carers cook, clean, remind, lift and worry, often around the clock. Their labour saves governments enormous sums, yet it rarely appears in economic statistics because no money changes hands.",
   "The cost, however, does not disappear. Carers are more likely to give up paid jobs, to suffer from stress and poor sleep, and to fall ill themselves. Many describe a quiet conflict between duty and exhaustion. In the absence of wider public help, some countries have begun to act: Spain offers family carers a monthly allowance, Japan runs subsidised day centres, and several cities fund short \"respite\" stays so that carers can rest.",
   "Economists argue that these small measures cut the long-term bill. If carers collapse, the state ends up paying far more for hospitals and nursing homes. The deeper point is cultural. A society that treats aging as a private misfortune will quietly punish the very people who hold it together. A society that treats it as shared work — through paid leave, training and fair wages — may find that both the old and their carers live better, and for longer."
  ],
  "zh": "全球正快速老龄化，到 2050 年每六人中就有一位超过 65 岁。目前绝大多数长期照护是由家人（尤其是女性）无偿承担的，这份劳动省下了巨额公共开支，却几乎不计入经济统计。照护者的代价是放弃工作、承受压力与睡眠不足、甚至自己生病。西班牙、日本等已开始提供津贴、日间照料与短期“喘息”服务。经济学家的观点是：若照护者垮掉，国家要付出更高的医院与养老院成本。更深一层是文化问题——把老龄化当作私人的不幸，会悄悄惩罚那些撑着家庭的人；把它当作共同的责任，老人与照护者才能活得更好、更久。",
